            <ArticleTitle>The use of epidural bupivacaine for the relief of childbirth pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Epidural anaesthesia is now a widely used method for pain relief in childbirth, particularly using the drug Bupivacaine. There are nevertheless differing opinions in the research literature about the advisability of its routine use. While it is clearly very effective in relieving labour pain, there are some consistent, troublesome patterns; for example, a strong association between epidural use and other interventions, such as instrumental delivery. Further, there are no clear answers from the research to date concerning the risks and benefits of epidural anaesthesia for infant and mother. Answers could be provided by randomized clinical trials, but meanwhile a conservative approach to its use is recommended for uncomplicated labours.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
